A `:value` binding on radio writes back the option that was clicked. The
override says so itself -- `onClick=func() { value = opt.label; change(opt.label) }` --
and its write of `value` is the binding's write-back.

The binding pass once guessed where the write-back went, and invented a
parameter on the callback. Compose's onClick is
`() -> Unit`, so the emitted Kotlin did not compile, and `opt` -- the whole
point of the loop -- never reached the write-back.

The write-back is `pick = opt.label`: the loop variable's label, in the loop. It used to
read `pick = "a"` and `pick = "b"` because a literal `options` was unrolled
here; Kotlin emits the loop now, so `opt` is what the assignment reads.

With no `direction`, the override takes its else-branch and lays the options
out in a Column. Both branches are fixtures because the injection used to stop
at the first match it walked into, which is the branch that is not taken; the
horizontal one is generate_android_radio_bind_horizontal.txtar.

deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `newValue ->` -- the injected callback takes no parameter; the clicked value comes from the loop variable, and a parameter here would not compile against Compose's `() -> Unit`
deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `onClick = \{\}` -- no RadioButton is left with an empty callback, which is what the write-back landing in the other branch looks like
-- vertical.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main node {
    var pick string = "a"
    radio(:value=pick) {
        choice(label="a")
        choice(label="b")
    }
    text(value=pick)
}

window {
    main
}
